A||||18||Eriamjh||Front Caliper/Brake Upgrade?||||1||
Z||000000||Eriamjh||11-23-2001||05:06 PM||||OK, I know you can put Grand Am front calipers on the rears and used the vented discs to improve rear stopping power (and eliminate non-working parking brake cables), but...<P>Is there a bolt on upgrade for the front calipers?  I know the fiero store sell a bolt on upgrade kit, but it's like $350.<P>Is there any cheap upgrade that doesn't require a ton of rework like new spindles?||||reg||1
Z||000001||pherder||11-23-2001||05:20 PM||||Shameless plug ...<P><A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum4/HTML/005774.html" TARGET=_blank>Try this</A><P>No resending in your old calipers for core refund and no finding a machine shop that will turn down your hubs.<P>Paul||||reg||6
Z||000002||Monkeyman||11-24-2001||06:21 AM||||Read Ogres cave article before you mess with the GA upgrade.  I did the 4 wheel GA swap on a previous Fiero a while back.  New Blazer M/C, new SS brake lines, FS kit on the front and parts store parts on the rear.  I didn't notice enough of a difference to make it worth the money.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Eriamjh||11-24-2001||08:53 AM||||The upgrade looks like and I'm sure the master cylinder makes a big improvement, but I'm already spending $1000 on the suspension repair and this upgrade is a little steep (read the first post to see I did NOT want to spend $350 for an upgrade).<P>Anyway, I found rebuilt front calipers for $60 each special order.  I guess I'll just go with them.<P>There are no other cheap bolt on upgrades, huh?<P>------------------<BR>Dan||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fierowrecker||11-24-2001||10:51 AM||||Hey Dan!<BR>The best advise I can give ya, Replace the calipers on all four corners (stock), and replace the hoses too...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||11-24-2001||12:47 PM||||we just covered a whole raft of stuff about GA parts in the last week. read back thru the last 5 days topics.<P>The choice of line is up to you, I prefer new OE style, but definatly change the lines while changing the calipers. The old lines are probably in tought shape and it's allot easier and somewhat cheaper to do it all together.<P>If the line jacket is cracked, especially if cracked to cording, then the line MUST be replaced. Once the cording is exposed, it's only a matter of time to line failure. <P>If you have to keep the old lines, DO NOT allow penetrating oil to soak on the rubber line! it can soak into the jacket and swell the lining, which ruins the lines.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mach10||11-24-2001||01:45 PM||||From what i understood, going to the GA brake upgrade ELIMINATES your e-brake... You are slapping FW calipers/rotors on the rear. FW calipers wouldn't have a provision for an e-brake....<P>Also: i would NEVER want to "improve" rear-stopping power like that. No-WAY. there's a reason that your brakes are biased to the front... unless you LIKE spinning out on highways. Fix up your OEM system, or else buy a REAL, properly engineered braking kit...||||reg||13
Z||000007||PBJ||11-24-2001||04:56 PM||||I AM SURE TO GET SLAMMED HERE FOR SAYING THIS BUT, ITS MY OPINION.  I have'nt run the numbers till I talked myself out of the swap.<P>But the fiero is not like other frt engines requiring heavy braking on frt and little on rear.  With light frt vehicle weight and heavy rear end, G/am brakes work well (I THINK GREAT) If done on all 4rs <P>I do agree with the concern about no E brake. This is a good arguing point (I doubt 1/8 of the fiero's on the road have working E brakes, they barely had a working one when new. again my opinion) but don't talk yourself out of the deal because of brake Bias, IT is just not an issue with our car.  <P>I followed the steps on all 4 corners incl new m/c and am again very happy with braking.<P>Most of the people who don't like G/A brakes did not do the whole swap or even done the swap, may not have even driven on with.<P>Now with all this said.  The recommendation made by others regarding putting the same $ into fixing your stock brakes with QUALITY parts Should give you good results too.<P>Thanks for listening.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mach10||11-24-2001||05:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by PBJ:<BR><B>I AM SURE TO GET SLAMMED HERE FOR SAYING THIS BUT, ITS MY OPINION.  I have'nt run the numbers till I talked myself out of the swap.<P>But the fiero is not like other frt engines requiring heavy braking on frt and little on rear.  With light frt vehicle weight and heavy rear end, G/am brakes work well (I THINK GREAT) If done on all 4rs <P>I do agree with the concern about no E brake. This is a good arguing point (I doubt 1/8 of the fiero's on the road have working E brakes, they barely had a working one when new. again my opinion) but don't talk yourself out of the deal because of brake Bias, IT is just not an issue with our car.  <P>I followed the steps on all 4 corners incl new m/c and am again very happy with braking.<P>Most of the people who don't like G/A brakes did not do the whole swap or even done the swap, may not have even driven on with.<P>Now with all this said.  The recommendation made by others regarding putting the same $ into fixing your stock brakes with QUALITY parts Should give you good results too.<P>Thanks for listening.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>As it is, in a straight line, the GA brakes will make little different except in stopping distance. In a straight line, it won't matter as much if the rear locks. Now, the weight transfer *IS* a little better with the ME design, but when you are on the brakes, all the weight shifts to the front. Especially in the 84-87 range, since the front-end tends to dive. This makes for some tricky braking if you do so in a corner, for whatever reason...<P>Again, I would never go to retro-fit from another car... I would spend the cash on a properly designed and tested unit. Just my 0.000042342c (after exchange)<P>||||reg||6
Z||000009||PBJ||11-24-2001||07:51 PM||||Retro fit from another car??<P>The 84-87 fiero<P>chevette frt brakes<BR>cammaro back brakes<P>pontiac 6000 cradle, lower control arm, rear suspension pretty much moved to the back of a p body.<P>chevette frt suspension.<P>I am sure the list goes on<P>The fiero was not it's own untill 88.  so RETRO-FIT???  Hmm.<P>But true, I really only do straight ahead breaking, the back end of Mid engine is always something to "hang on to" when they do get loose in a corner.<p>[This message has been edited by PBJ (edited 11-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||DKOV||11-24-2001||08:40 PM||||Why loose your e-brake to upgrade your primary braking system?<P>Just get the Held Motorsports kit in 12 or 13 inch...<P> <IMG SRC="http://heldmotorsports.com/images/13fbrk.jpg"> <P>And call it BEAUTIFUL!!!<P>Quite possibly the best looking hop-up on my car!!!<P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000011||Mach10||11-24-2001||08:50 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by PBJ:<BR><B>Retro fit from another car??<P>The 84-87 fiero<P>chevette frt brakes<BR>cammaro back brakes<P>pontiac 6000 cradle, lower control arm, rear suspension pretty much moved to the back of a p body.<P>chevette frt suspension.<P>I am sure the list goes on<P>The fiero was not it's own untill 88.  so RETRO-FIT???  Hmm.<P>But true, I really only do straight ahead breaking, the back end of Mid engine is always something to "hang on to" when they do get loose in a corner.<P>[This message has been edited by PBJ (edited 11-24-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> You know what I mean  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>The biases have been specifically set for the combination of caliper-rotor-MC set on the Fiero by someone who spent WAY too much time doing Calculus in school. A normally working system has a certain balance that you can and will upset by changing the variables. <P>Now, *IF* someone were to successfully calibrate the system, test it, and tell the rest of us how to do it, I might have more faith in it. Has the "Held" system gone through such R&D? Probably... Otherwise they'd get sued  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <BR>I'd go for that one  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>As it is, I do NOT ****  with my brakes. My engine can catch fire, My tranny explode, or my wheels can wear themselves bald from odd alignment, but there's no way in HELL that I'd EVER be less than 100% confident in my brakes.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||10
Z||000012||Her86GT||11-24-2001||09:05 PM||||Anyone who wants to drive a sweet car with a Grand Am upgrade can check out mine.  I love it!  Besides, I'm gonna need 'em because PBJ is throwing in a Caddy 4.9 into my baby!!!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mycar2sm.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||2birds||11-24-2001||09:12 PM||||I'm not an owner of a Held (or any other) brake upgrade, but isn't that an adjustable proportioning valve in the upper left corner of the picture of that kit (to the left of the master cylinder)?  Unless it's preset, seems to me that you have to set up your own brake bias anyway (trial and error?), even with such a high dollar, pre-engineered kit.  A new prop valve is only $40-$80 from Summit Racing, according to my catalog.  And with the GA rotors, you should still be able to use your original wheels.  Doesn't the Held kit require larger diameter wheels?<BR>Personally, I think mine stops just fine with the OEM setup.  I only have Goodridge braided hoses, because at $80 a set (Fiero Store, 1995), they were cheaper than new, store brand rubber ones.  But, then, I don't race mine, either, unless you count The Daily Jacksonville/I-95 Rush Hour Grand Prix.||||reg||1
Z||000014||BV MotorSports||11-24-2001||10:46 PM||||I have had the GA upgrade on my 87GT for about 2 yrs now. I have had ZERO trouble and NO maint issues at all. JR from kitcarman.com<BR>made my front "hubs" and I had all 4 rotors turned and had a uni-directional finish cut on them right out of the boxes. Not one rotor was straight out of the box. I also added Performance Friction carbon metallic pads too. BTW not a single squeak or rattle from my brakes after 2yrs of hard use!<BR>I AutoX, road race and drag race this car plus I am pretty aggressive driver (love a twisty road!) and the brakes are great. i would like to add the larger brakes to the rear soon. I had a little mishap where the car took off all by itself (no e-brake remember?) and I have been concerned every since. I think the larger brake use s-10 4X4 rotors and 3rd gen F-body calipers with a caliper mounting bracket and modified e-brake levers. I'll be doing this soon! Too bad I tossed the stock e-brake cables long ago.<BR>the GA brakes work great and are WAY cheaper to maintain vrs the stock brakes. Hope this helps.<P>Steven<P>------------------<BR>87 Fiero GT 14.9/ 89mph<BR>94 Formula WS6 w/ NOS 12.4 /118mph<BR>87 Conquest TSi <BR>87 Conquest TSi 406 SBC V8!<BR>85.5 Starion ESi||||reg||1
Z||000015||Will||11-24-2001||11:20 PM||||[RANT]<P>This isn't the first time I've heard someone bellyaching about a "properly engineered kit".<BR>Just what exactly constitutes proper engineering?<BR>Am I somehow unable to "properly engineer" my brakes because the word "incorporated" doesn't follow my name?<P>[/RANT]<P>I've had the GA brakes on all four wheels for a while, and I like it a lot, but IT IS NOT FOR A NOVICE.<BR>I thought the balance was quite good. I was running 235front/245rear tires. I'm going to 215 front/255 rear shortly, which will bring the tire sizes more in line with the car's weight distribution. The different tire sizes will also allow EVEN MORE rear brake force to be used controllably, in a corner.<P>One thing to consider is that GM "properly engineered" the Fiero brakes for the average idiot on the road. Translation: way too little rear braking force so that there is never a chance under any conditions that some poor shmuck could stand on the brakes at the wrong time--causing the back end of his car to beat him around the corner--then sue GM for designing the car poorly, when his driving skills are really at fault.<P>Someone experienced in the ways of high performance driving can make good use of a LOT more rear braking force than the stock setup had.<P>Ever dial the car out to 10 10ths on a merge ramp then hit the brakes and get back on the gas? In the wet?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Tons of fun driving sideways down the road.||||reg||1
Z||000016||2birds||11-25-2001||02:07 AM||||quote "Ever dial the car out to 10 10ths on a merge ramp then hit the brakes and get back on the gas? In the wet?    Tons of fun driving sideways down the road."<P>Done that (on ice).  Hit the mailbox.  Don't want to do it again.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000017||DKOV||11-25-2001||09:40 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 2birds:<BR><B>I'm not an owner of a Held (or any other) brake upgrade, but isn't that an adjustable proportioning valve in the upper left corner of the picture of that kit (to the left of the master cylinder)?  Unless it's preset, seems to me that you have to set up your own brake bias anyway (trial and error?), even with such a high dollar, pre-engineered kit.  A new prop valve is only $40-$80 from Summit Racing, according to my catalog.  And with the GA rotors, you should still be able to use your original wheels.  Doesn't the Held kit require larger diameter wheels?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>A couple of things... Yes, that is a proportioning valve and it sets from factory preset to rear bias.  It's for fine tuning and the Fiero brake system is VERY bias to the front from the factory.  Just corner and brake at the same time and you'll notice that right off the bat...<P>Second, the bigger wheels are only if you choose the 13 inch kit.  The 12 inch kit works with the stock rims.<P>Finally, The stock Fiero brakes have let me down a few times on the track and a couple of times on the public roads.  Once, just because I was trying to stop in the rain from 15 MPH!!!  I decided that it was time for NEW brakes!<P>I really love my big brakes.  Wether you choose to do a GA swap or run the Held setup, it's a good idea to get the STOP right...  Especially with all the extra GO most of us have in these cars  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000018||lowCG||11-26-2001||08:37 PM||||Yeah Will,I did that inside a tunnel once,what a rush!<BR>I struggled through installing 4 piston BMW calipers,and 11" Subaru rotors on my car,now it stops from 140 like it used to from 45 with the stock stuff.<BR>And I haven't even bled the rears yet after the master cylinder/front caliper install,I might try to use a different spring in the proportioning valve in order to get a closer balance,since I now have nearly twice the piston area as before.<BR>Cost me about $400 with new teflon lines,rebuild kits for the calipers+lots of screwing around.||||reg||1
